The CSS` corner-shape` property changes the curve of a corner without changing its size.` border-radius` still sets how far the corner reaches along each edge, and` corner-shape` picks the path the edge follows inside that area.


For years, a bevelled tag meant writing` clip-path` polygon coordinates by hand, and a smooth app-icon corner meant pulling in an SVG mask. Both workarounds tend to break in the same place: the box shadow, which gets cut off or has to be faked.


This article covers the syntax, what each keyword draws, how to set shapes per corner, why shadows behave better than they do with` clip-path` , how to animate between shapes, and when you need a fallback.


## Key Takeaways


- ` corner-shape` only takes effect when` border-radius` resolves to a non-zero value, and its initial value,` round` , draws the same corner` border-radius` draws by itself.
- Each keyword is a named` superellipse()` value:` square` is` infinity` ,` squircle` is` 2` ,` round` is` 1` ,` bevel` is` 0` ,` scoop` is` -1` , and` notch` is` -infinity` .
- Borders, outlines, box shadows and backgrounds follow the reshaped corner.` clip-path` behaves differently and cuts the shadow off with everything else outside the clip region.
- Chrome and Edge ship` corner-shape` from version 139. Firefox and Safari have it only in Nightly, Technology Preview and beta builds, so the feature is not Baseline.


## What CSS corner-shape Does


` corner-shape` reshapes the area that` border-radius` reserves at each corner. With no radius, there is nothing to reshape. MDN’s[border-radius reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/border-radius) makes the same point with an example: a corner set to a border-radius of 0 gets no corner shape applied.


Think of` border-radius` and` corner-shape` as two separate controls.` border-radius: 2rem` reserves a 2rem region at the corner.` corner-shape` decides whether the edge crosses that region as a curve, a straight cut or an inward scoop. Per the[MDN corner-shape reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/corner-shape) , every corner longhand starts at` round` , so adding the property changes nothing until you set a value.


## The Six Keywords and superellipse()


Every` corner-shape` keyword is shorthand for a` superellipse()` value. Positive arguments bulge outward and get squarer as the number rises. Zero draws a straight diagonal between the two ends of the radius. Negative arguments curve inward toward the centre of the box. The[<corner-shape-value> page on MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/corner-shape-value) defines` squircle` as a convex curve between round and square, equal to` superellipse(2)` , and` bevel` as a straight diagonal that is neither convex nor concave, equal to` superellipse(0)` .


Keyword` superellipse()` equivalent Corner looks like


` square`` superellipse(infinity)` A sharp right angle


` squircle`` superellipse(2)` A soft curve, squarer than round


` round`` superellipse(1)` The standard elliptical arc


` bevel`` superellipse(0)` A straight diagonal cut


` scoop`` superellipse(-1)` A concave arc


` notch`` superellipse(-infinity)` A square cut inward


The grammar in the[CSS Borders and Box Decorations Level 4 draft](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-borders-4/#corner-shaping) accepts a single argument: a number,` infinity` or` -infinity` , as the[corner-start-start-shape reference on MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/corner-start-start-shape) also shows. To compare all six side by side, put them on six sibling cards:


```text
.  shapes   {   display  :   grid  ;   grid-template-columns  : repeat(  3  ,   8rem  );   gap  :   1rem  ; }
.  shapes   >   *   {
aspect-ratio  :   1  ;
background  : #  4f46e5  ;
border-radius  :   2rem  ;
}
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  1  ) { corner-shape:   square  ; }
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  2  ) { corner-shape: squircle; }
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  3  ) { corner-shape:   round  ; }
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  4  ) { corner-shape:   bevel  ; }
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  5  ) { corner-shape: scoop; }
.  shapes   >   :  nth-child  (  6  ) { corner-shape: notch; }
```


## Why border-radius Can’t Draw a Squircle


` border-radius` only draws elliptical arcs, so the curvature changes abruptly at the point where the corner meets the straight edge.` corner-shape: squircle` replaces that arc with a superellipse that leaves the edge more gradually. The result is the softer corner common in modern app icons.


```text
.  icon          {   width  :   6rem  ;   aspect-ratio  :   1  ;   border-radius  :   30%  ;   background  : #  0ea5e9  ; }
.  icon  .  smooth   { corner-shape: squircle; }   /* same as superellipse(2) */
```


At the same radius, a squircle looks less rounded than` round` , because a higher superellipse value sits closer to the square. If the corner looks too tight, increase the radius before you reach for a custom` superellipse()` value.


## Per-Corner Shapes with Multi-Value Syntax and Longhands


` corner-shape` takes one to four values in the same corner order as` border-radius` . One value sets all four corners. With two values, the first sets top-left and bottom-right and the second sets top-right and bottom-left. With four values, the order runs clockwise from top-left. For finer control, MDN lists physical longhands such as` corner-top-left-shape` , logical longhands such as` corner-start-start-shape` , and side shorthands such as` corner-top-shape` and` corner-inline-start-shape` .


For a breadcrumb chip that points back toward the start of the line, use the logical side shorthand so the bevel flips in right-to-left text.[corner-inline-start-shape](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/corner-inline-start-shape) sets both corners on the inline-start edge, which are` corner-start-start-shape` and` corner-end-start-shape` .


```text
.  crumb   {
border-radius  :   0.75rem  ;
corner-inline-start-shape:   bevel  ;   /* LTR: same as corner-shape: bevel round round bevel */
padding-inline  :   1.25rem   0.75rem  ;
}
```


The extra start padding on` .crumb` is there for a reason.` corner-shape` changes how the box is painted, not how its content is laid out. Text and child elements still sit in the rectangular box and can spill into a bevelled or scooped corner unless padding keeps them clear.


## Borders and box-shadow Follow the Shape


` corner-shape` reshapes the box itself, so the shadow, border, outline and background all follow the new corner.` clip-path` , by contrast, cuts away everything outside the clip region,` box-shadow` included. Per MDN, the corner shape also applies to` overflow` clipping and` backdrop-filter` .


```text
/* Before: clip-path removes the shadow along with the cut corners */
.  ticket-clip   {
--cut:   1rem  ;
box-shadow  :   0   8px   24px   rgb(  0   0   0   /   .25  );
clip-path  : polygon(  0   0  , calc(  100%   -   var(--cut))   0  ,   100%   var(--cut),
100%   100%  , var(--cut)   100%  ,   0   calc(  100%   -   var(--cut)));
}


/* After: shadow, border and outline trace the bevel */
.  ticket   {
border-radius  :   1rem  ;
corner-shape:   square   bevel  ;   /* TL+BR square, TR+BL bevelled */
border  :   2px   solid   #  f59e0b  ;
box-shadow  :   0   8px   24px   rgb(  0   0   0   /   .25  );
}
```


The usual` clip-path` workaround is to wrap the element and put` filter: drop-shadow()` on the wrapper.` corner-shape` needs neither the wrapper nor the filter.[clip-path](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/clip-path) is still the right tool for arbitrary outlines, as covered in[getting creative with CSS shape functions](https://blog.openreplay.com/creative-css-shape-functions/) and[drawing layout-friendly shapes with xywh()](https://blog.openreplay.com/drawing-layout-friendly-shapes-css-xywh/) .


## Animating Between Shapes on Hover


` corner-shape` is animatable, so a regular` transition` can morph one shape into another. MDN notes that animations interpolate through the` superellipse()` equivalents, which lets keywords animate smoothly into each other.


```text
.  btn   {
border-radius  :   1rem  ;
corner-shape: squircle;
transition  : corner-shape   250ms   ease  ;
}
.  btn  :  hover  ,   .  btn  :  focus-visible   { corner-shape:   bevel  ; }


@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
.  btn   {   transition  :   none  ; }
}
```


Avoid designing around the exact in-between frames of a` corner-shape` transition. WebKit’s[Technology Preview 252 notes](https://webkit.org/blog/18304/release-notes-for-safari-technology-preview-252/) mention a fix that brings outset-curve interpolation in line with the updated spec algorithm, which means intermediate frames can still differ between engines.


## Fallback with @supports (corner-shape: squircle)


Browsers that don’t support` corner-shape` ignore the declaration and draw ordinary rounded corners from the existing` border-radius` . A squircle card or a soft bevel therefore falls back to a normal rounded rectangle, and most uses need no extra code.


Chrome added the property in[Chrome 139](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/new-in-chrome-139) , and Edge supports it from the same version. Firefox supports it only in Nightly builds, starting with 158, according to the[Firefox Nightly release notes](https://www.firefox.com/en-US/firefox/160.0a1/releasenotes/) . Safari added support in[Technology Preview 251](https://webkit.org/blog/18194/release-notes-for-safari-technology-preview-251/) , and the[Safari 27.3 beta release notes](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/safari-release-notes/safari-27_3-release-notes) list it too, but it isn’t in a stable Safari release yet. MDN lists the feature as Limited availability, so check its compat table before you ship.


The fallback stops being acceptable when the shape depends on` corner-shape` itself. A large radius combined with` bevel` makes a diamond in supporting browsers, but an unsupported browser renders the same radius as an ellipse. In that case, gate the large radius behind[@supports](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@supports) :


```text
.  badge   {   border-radius  :   0.5rem  ; }


@supports (corner-shape:   bevel  ) {
.  badge   {   border-radius  :   50%  ; corner-shape:   bevel  ; }
}
```


A few cases where the property doesn’t do what you might expect:


- A` border-radius` that resolves to 0 means` corner-shape` has no effect.
- Content stays rectangular. Pad it away from bevelled or scooped corners.
- When the shapes of opposite corners would overlap, MDN documents that the browser constrains them.
- Per MDN,` corner-shape` has no effect when` border-shape` is set.


## Conclusion


` corner-shape` turns` border-radius` into a two-part control: the radius sets the size of the corner and the keyword sets its curve. Because unsupported browsers fall back to plain rounded corners, squircles and soft bevels are safe to add to existing components now. Keep` @supports` for designs where the outline itself, such as a diamond or a notched badge, would turn into an ellipse without the property. A good first change is to add` corner-shape: squircle` to a card or icon that already uses` border-radius` , then compare it with the original.


## FAQs


Can I use superellipse() values other than the six keyword equivalents?


Yes. superellipse() accepts any number, plus infinity and -infinity. superellipse(0.5) draws a curve between bevel and round, superellipse(1.5) sits between round and squircle, and values like -2 or -3 give deeper concave scoops. The same value can look different on two elements if their box size or border-radius differs, because the shape is drawn inside each element's own radius region.


Does corner-shape work with elliptical or percentage border-radius values?


Yes. corner-shape draws its curve inside whatever region border-radius computes for each corner, including percentage radii and elliptical radii set with the slash syntax, such as border-radius: 2rem / 1rem. A bevel on an elliptical radius becomes a longer, shallower diagonal cut. A squircle on an elliptical radius becomes a stretched superellipse, so changing the radius changes the proportions of the shape.


How do I clip an image or child element to a squircle or bevelled card?


Set overflow: hidden or overflow: clip on the card. MDN lists overflow clipping among the things that follow the corner shape, so child content gets clipped to the squircle or bevel instead of the rectangle. Without overflow clipping, children paint in the rectangular box and can show in the cut-away corners. corner-shape is not inherited, so children never pick up the parent's shape automatically.


